---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import BenchTable from "../components/BenchTable.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/OGShawnLee/pino-lang";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Performance"
  description="Compiled with --release, Pino runs in the same range as Go and Rust, and faster than C#, Bun and Python on every benchmark except string building."
>
  <Hero>
    <Fragment slot="title">Performance</Fragment>
    <p slot="lead">
      Ten benchmarks plus <code>hello</code>, using the same algorithm in Pino, Go, Rust, C#, Bun and Python, with
      checked output.
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <Section label="Three ways to run" id="modes">
    <Fragment slot="title">How Pino runs your code</Fragment>
    <ul>
      <li><code>pino run</code> runs on the VM, with no C compiler needed.</li>
      <li><code>pino build</code> compiles native code through TinyCC, which compiles instantly with no optimizer.</li>
      <li>
        <code>pino build --release</code> compiles native code through GCC or Clang with <code>-O2</code>, which is the
        speed number.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Benchmarks" id="results">
    <Fragment slot="title">Results</Fragment>
    <BenchTable
      caption="Benchmark times in milliseconds"
      rows={[
        "hello",
        "fib",
        "binary-trees",
        "nbody",
        "spectral-norm",
        "mandelbrot",
        "sort",
        "word-count",
        "string-build",
        "regex",
        "lexer",
      ]}
      columns={[
        { key: "pino-vm", label: "Pino VM", pino: true },
        { key: "pino-c", label: "Pino (TinyCC)", pino: true },
        { key: "pino-gcc", label: "Pino (gcc)", pino: true },
        { key: "go", label: "Go" },
        { key: "rust", label: "Rust" },
        { key: "csharp", label: "C#" },
        { key: "bun", label: "Bun" },
        { key: "python", label: "Python" },
      ]}
    />
    <p class="text">
      Compiled with <code>--release</code>, Pino runs in the same range as Go and Rust, and faster than C#, Bun and
      Python on every benchmark except string building. The sizes are small on purpose so the suite runs in a few
      minutes on any machine, which puts Go, Rust and Pino with gcc close to their startup time: the table shows
      they're in the same league, not which one wins.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      <strong>Known weak spots:</strong> building a string with <code>s = s + x</code> is slow in native Pino today,
      because the runtime copies on every append; a fix is next. The VM takes about half a second to start and is
      slower than Python today.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Goals, not measurements" id="goals">
    <Fragment slot="title">Goals</Fragment>
    <ul>
      <li>String building with gcc in the Go/Rust range after the fix.</li>
      <li>VM startup under 0.1 s, and the VM within 1.5–3× of Python's time.</li>
      <li>In 0.5, native Pino within about 1–2× of Rust on every benchmark.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text">
      Reference counting has a cost, so Pino isn't expected to beat hand-tuned C or Rust in general.
    </p>
    <p class="more"><a href={`${repo}/blob/main/benchmarks/README.md`}>How it was measured</a></p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .text {
    max-width: 40rem;
    color: var(--soft);
  }
  .note {
    max-width: 36rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--soft);
  }
  .more a {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor;
    padding-bottom: 0.15rem;
  }
  .more a::after {
    content: " →";
  }
  .more a:hover {
    color: var(--pine);
  }
  code {
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }
  ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  li {
    padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1.4rem;
    text-indent: -1.4rem;
  }
  li::before {
    content: "— ";
    color: var(--pine);
  }
</style>
